# M30 — rule 9b's UNREGISTERED branch made unreachable.
#
# The third of rule 9b's three branches, and the second one M20 was tagged against but could
# not kill. Same reasoning as M29: the control existed, the mutation naming it could not
# reach it, and the honest fix is a mutation per branch rather than a shorter catcher list.
#
# WHAT THE BRANCH IS FOR, and why it is the one that closed a real gap. Before the port, five
# protected reference tables carried sentinel rows that NOTHING CHECKED — waived out of the
# old rule 9 by an exemption row. A protected table with no `user_id` column and no
# `posture_sentinel_row` entry was SKIPPED. That matters because an empty forced-RLS table
# does not deny at all (V18): the policy qual is never evaluated, so a denial test over it
# passes while proving nothing. Unregistered therefore has to be a FAILURE, not a skip, and
# it is deliberately not waivable through `posture_exempt_table` any more.
#
# With the branch unreachable, such a table falls through to the counting branch, where
# `format('%I', NULL)` produces an EXECUTE that cannot run — so the rule reports "could not
# be evaluated" instead of naming the unregistered table, and the control's assertion on its
# own fragment goes red.
id: M30
title: rule 9b's unregistered-table branch made unreachable, so a protected table with no sentinel is skipped rather than reported
expect: caught
proves: rule 9b treats an unregistered protected table as a failure rather than a skip, which is the gap the port closed and the reason an empty forced table cannot be relied on to deny (V18)
expect-test: [M30] raises ST099 for a protected table with no user_id column at all

file: supabase/migrations/20261021140500_verify_security_posture.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
      elsif r.reg_col is null then
@@REPLACE
      elsif false and r.reg_col is null then
@@END
